[{"id":68536,"sender_id":147,"sender_full_name":"Dmitri Pavlov","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7c66d1b7cad17c58076e0f3d13220d63?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Is.20there.20a.20locally.20cartesian.20closed.20category.20of.20topological.E2.80.A6/near/68536\">#1</a> Some categories of topological spaces, such as <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/compactly%20generated%20topological%20spaces\">compactly generated topological spaces</a>, are <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cartesian%20closed\">cartesian closed</a>.</p>\n<p>Is there a convenient/nice category of topological spaces that is <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/locally%20cartesian%20closed\">locally cartesian closed</a>?</p>\n<p>For instance, are <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/%CE%94-generated%20topological%20spaces\">Δ-generated topological spaces</a> locally cartesian closed?</p>","timestamp":1529279232,"subject":"Is there a locally cartesian closed category of topological…","stream_id":5,"display_recipient":"nLab General Discussions","reactions":[]},{"id":68537,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Is.20there.20a.20locally.20cartesian.20closed.20category.20of.20topological.E2.80.A6/near/68537\">#2</a> The closest thing to an answer that I know of is that if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0144em;\">K</span></span></span></span> is the category of not-necessarily-weak-Hausdorff <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/k-spaces\">k-spaces</a>, and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span></span></span></span> are k-spaces that <em>are</em> weak Hausdorff, then the pullback functor <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{K}/B \\to \\mathcal{K}/A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0144em;\">K</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0144em;\">K</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span> has a right adjoint.  This is what May and Sigurdsson used in their book <em>Parametrized homotopy theory</em>.</p>\n<p>If you give up on the desire that your category be a subcategory of <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Top\">Top</a>, then there are closely related categories that are <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/quasitopoi\">quasitopoi</a> and hence lccc, such as <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/subsequential%20spaces\">subsequential spaces</a>, <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/convergence%20spaces\">convergence spaces</a>, and <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/pseudotopological%20spaces\">pseudotopological spaces</a>.</p>","timestamp":1529280310,"subject":"Is there a locally cartesian closed category of topological…","stream_id":5,"display_recipient":"nLab General Discussions","reactions":[]},{"id":68572,"sender_id":147,"sender_full_name":"Dmitri Pavlov","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7c66d1b7cad17c58076e0f3d13220d63?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Is.20there.20a.20locally.20cartesian.20closed.20category.20of.20topological.E2.80.A6/near/68572\">#3</a> Thanks!  I transplanted your description into the article <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/compactly%20generated%20topological%20space\">compactly generated topological space</a>.</p>","timestamp":1529346332,"subject":"Is there a locally cartesian closed category of topological…","stream_id":5,"display_recipient":"nLab General Discussions","reactions":[]}]